t’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 \\\\ {(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′} & {(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡x-({cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368177 13194139535819 true] [17592186368176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368178 13194139535819 true] [17592186368176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368179 13194139535819 true] [17592186368176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368180 13194139535819 true] [17592186368176 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535819 true] [17592186368176 1041 "field-1" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368176 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368179 13194139535819 true] [17592186368176 1110 #uuid "e8587786-ae81-59b0-988e-39a832083828" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368177 1034 "-1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368177 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535819 true] [17592186368177 1111 #uuid "79c8d9af-82c6-5ecf-b9f7-0bdfbc22d681" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368178 1034 "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535819 true] [17592186368178 1111 #uuid "090180d9-9124-56a0-9cf5-99a7cf970b26" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368179 1034 "-1-\\operato