 "c4383592-63fe-566f-8f01-b00301d125c4" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368187 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368188 13194139535819 true] [17592186368187 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368193 13194139535819 true] [17592186368187 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368196 13194139535819 true] [17592186368187 1089 "q-334120" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368187 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535819 true] [17592186368187 1100 "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}+1,\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},  \nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368187 1113 #uuid "33c7e127-d81f-5cec-b0b0-ddcb001acc95" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368187 1145 "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}x & {x}^{3}+1 \\\\ (x)^{′} & ({x}^{3}+1)^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}x & {x}^{3}+1 \\\\ 1 & 3{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =x\\cdot (3{x}^{2})-({x}^{3}+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}-{x}^{3}-1 \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368188 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368189 13194139535819 true] [17592186368188 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368190 13194139535819 true] [17592186368188 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368191 13194139535819 true] [17592186368188 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368192 13194139535819 true] [17592186368188 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535819 true] [17592186368188 1041 "field-1" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368188 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368190 13194139535819 true] [17592186368188 1110 #uuid "7280a9c3-8e09-568f-a4f0-61d0b3a6c7f3" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368189 1034 "{x}^{3}-x" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368189 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535819 true] [17592186368189 1111 #uuid "55ed9799-3877-5b9f-9b04-4b3c6f1d6103" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368190 1034 "2{x}^{3}-1" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368190 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535819 true] [17592186368190 1111 #uuid "9229e6b5-1304-5e5c-84ae-c85da7c394a5" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368191 1034 "{x}^{2}+1" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368191 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535819 true] [17592186368191 1111 #uuid "2a75cf0f-8afc-5ae0-b547-505a4aa6f37d" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368192 1034 "0" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368192 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535819 true] [17592186368192 1111 #uuid "3c62095a-95a8-5108-9dc8-d89110ace09f" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368193 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368194 13194139535819 true] [17592186368193 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368195 13194139535819 true] [17592186368193 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535819 true] [17592186368193 1041 "field-2" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368193 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368195 13194139535819 true] [17592186368193 1110 #uuid "09cef95f-da8e-5609-81ec-4c8a7e980ddb" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368194 1034 "zero for all $x\\in R$" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368194 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535819 true] [17592186368194 1111 #uuid "611b5e18-a429-570a-a443-c4435e5b9415" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368195 1034 "nonzero for at least one $x\\in R$" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368195 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535819 true] [17592186368195 1111 #uuid "123797b3-47e8-509c-98a9-cec833ce5553" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368197 13194139535819 true] [17592186368196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368198 13194139535819 true] [17592186368196 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535819 true] [17592186368196 1041 "field-3" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368196 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368198 13194139535819 true] [17592186368196 1110 #uuid "55779daa-c3c2-5448-8197-159ddfb6239c" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368197 1034 "dependent" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368197 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535819 true] [17592186368197 1111 #uuid "a7ffce77-52d5-54df-bb83-73d19e6ffa8c" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368198 1034 "independent" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368198 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535819 true] [17592186368198 1111 #uuid "61462246-c168-51f4-813e-9db55f2425d1" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368199 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368200 13194139535819 true] [17592186368199 1089 "q-334266" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368199 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535819 true] [17592186368199 1100 "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+4y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368199 1113 #uuid "a5e7a1fb-5172-520d-b15a-ca0c65b53d2a" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368199 1145 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 2-3x\\cdot 2x+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3)-3x(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-3{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ 2x & 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139535819 true] [17592186368200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368201 13194139535819 true] [17592186368200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368202 13194139535819 true] [17592186368200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368203 13194139535819 true] [17592186368200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368204 13194139535819 true] [17592186368200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368205 13194139535819 true] [17592186368200 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535819 true] [17592186368200 1041 "selection" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368200 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368201 13194139535819 true] [17592186368200 1110 #uuid "dc61a57e-3344-51ec-9204-18f598921f20" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368201 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368201 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535819 true] [17592186368201 1111 #uuid "b17b0b91-cc18-5e30-8a24-a2e200472dab" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368202 1034 "I only" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368202 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535819 true] [17592186368202 1111 #uuid "bc046aaa-de2e-5335-8a8d-cf4dac702f7d" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368203 1034 "None" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368203 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535819 true] [17592186368203 1111 #uuid "91732f5a-9ce2-538e-bb65-5e994b64a0a9" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368204 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368204 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535819 true] [17592186368204 1111 #uuid "e768bee1-8a7b-5a5f-bf2a-ebd37224e2ed" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368205 1034 "II only" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368205 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535819 true] [17592186368205 1111 #uuid "947fd302-9ab8-5c1e-95cc-a28929c9bd7b" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368206 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368207 13194139535819 true] [17592186368206 1089 "q-334278" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368206 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535819 true] [17592186368206 1100 "$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." 13194139535819 true] [17592186368206 1113 #uuid "e5ef7138-6ef1-5117-966e-d0b3724a9ae1" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368206 1145 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)) \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535819 true] [17592186368207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368208 13194139535819 true] [17592186368207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368209 13194139535819 true] [17592186368207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368210 13194139535819 true] [17592186368207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368211 13194139535819 true] [17592186368207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368212 13194139535819 true] [17592186368207 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535819 true] [17592186368207 1041 "selection" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368207 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368211 13194139535819 true] [17592186368207 1110 #uuid "9b182d26-d370-5812-a41e-320a46e74f2f" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368208 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368208 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535819 true] [17592186368208 1111 #uuid "a8890d62-0152-5bac-9882-38e908e64b5a" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368209 1034 "I only" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368209 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535819 true] [17592186368209 1111 #uuid "975d659c-b43b-5336-854a-84d35d30628b" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368210 1034 "None" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368210 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535819 true] [17592186368210 1111 #uuid "14cf47e7-f952-5a03-bfeb-8cd8c8ca6e18" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368211 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368211 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535819 true] [17592186368211 1111 #uuid "c25cbc44-908b-5a5a-8c35-894e8c8f6398" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368212 1034 "II only" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368212 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535819 true] [17592186368212 1111 #uuid "ca187e84-a661-58a3-9890-774b8e9d5a9d" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368213 1017 #edb/ref 17592186368214 13194139535819 true] [17592186368213 1089 "q-64747" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368213 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535819 true] [17592186368213 1100 "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.$" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368213 1113 #uuid "2d6d6775-8a16-53f5-823c-f560d775f277" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368213 1145 "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{x}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =({e}^{x}){(x{e}^{x})}^{′}-(x{e}^{x}){({e}^{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})-(x{e}^{x})({e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x}-x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368215 13194139535819 true] [17592186368214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368216 13194139535819 true] [17592186368214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368217 13194139535819 true] [17592186368214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368218 13194139535819 true] [17592186368214 1000 #edb/ref 17592186368219 13194139535819 true] [17592186368214 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535819 true] [17592186368214 1041 "selection" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368214 1104 #edb/ref 17592186368215 13194139535819 true] [17592186368214 1110 #uuid "569512a0-403c-541f-b2e6-367c987dcfc0" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368215 1034 "{e}^{2x}" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368215 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535819 true] [17592186368215 1111 #uuid "2ca1b06c-f889-5ffa-a045-40ded7f868a9" 13194139535819 true] [17592186368216 1034 "2" 131941395358